     light parchment — while the land, which did have a dark rule, went
     near-black. The result was a black continent on a white sea, in a
     dark page. Half a theme looks like a bug because it is one.
     The site's own tokens now carry it, and the dark rules are written
     twice the way styles.css does it: once for the media query and once
     for an explicit [data-theme="dark"], because this site has a toggle
     as well as a system preference. */
  .smap { margin: 14px 0 6px; }
  .smap :global(svg.sm) { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto;
              /* True aspect in the viewBox, height capped here — see
                 lib/surname-map.js, where clamping it squashed Belgium. */
              max-height: 460px;
              background: var(--panel, #EDEAE3);
              border: 1px solid var(--rule, #D8D3C8); border-radius: 2px; }
  .smap :global(.sm-land) { fill: #D5CFC2; stroke: #BDB6A6; stroke-width: .5; }
  .smap :global(.sm-c circle) { fill: var(--accent, #2F6F4E); fill-opacity: .78;
                       stroke: var(--panel, #fff); stroke-width: 1; }
  .smap :global(.sm-u circle) { fill: none; stroke: var(--ink-3, #7C8A91);
